<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    TruePad — the app's privacy manifest.

    THREE SEPARATE QUESTIONS, answered separately, because Apple asks them
    separately and conflating them is how a manifest becomes decorative:

      1. Does the developer COLLECT data?          No.
      2. Does the app TRACK?                       No.
      3. Does the app call a REQUIRED-REASON API?  Yes — one category.

    (1) and (2) are not asserted because TruePad feels private. They are asserted
    because the shipping binary has no egress path: it links no networking
    framework, and imports zero networking, cloud or tracking symbols. A pad, a
    message and a receive code all exist ON DEVICE; none of them is collected by
    anyone. Data existing on a device is not data collected by a developer, and
    only the second is what this file declares.

    (3) is declared because it is TRUE, not because it is safe. TruePad makes
    three stat-family calls, all in TruePadStorage/DarwinFs.swift and all on paths
    inside the app's own container:

      · `stat` in the read path — a regular file, reached through a symlink or
        not, reads normally. This one FOLLOWS, deliberately, so this edition and
        Android answer alike.
      · `lstat` in the not-a-regular-file branch — a path that exists but is not a
        regular file must not be reported as absent, because absence is the state
        that permits a second handoff.
      · `lstat` in `exists(_:)` — whether a path is there, WITHOUT following. A
        `stat` that follows would let a `destroyed.json` symlinked to a deleted
        target read as absent, and a destroyed pair become usable again.

    Neither reads a timestamp. Apple's category is defined by the API, not by the
    field used, so the declaration is made on the API. The approved reason is
    C617.1: metadata of files inside the app container.

    NOTHING ELSE IS DECLARED. The other required-reason categories — system boot
    time, disk space, active keyboard, user defaults — are absent from the
    shipping source, and `tests/…` guards that they stay absent rather than this
    file being padded with reasons that describe nothing.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>

    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>

    <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
    <array/>

    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>C617.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
